m_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Franjo Tuđman Bridge (Dubrovnik)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.668269,"lo":18.079419,"d":1262,"ds":"The Franjo Tuđman Bridge (Croatian: Most dr. Franja Tuđmana) is a cable-stayed bridge carrying the D8 state road at the western approach to Dubrovnik, Croatia across Rijeka Dubrovačka near Port of Gruž. The original bridge design was developed in 1989; however, construction was stopped at the onset of the Croatian War of Independence.","wpu":"Franjo Tuđman Bridge (Dubrovnik)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Franjo_Tu%C4%91man_bridge%2C_Dubrovnik.jpg/960px-Franjo_Tu%C4%91man_bridge%2C_Dubrovnik.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lozica, Croatia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.6725,"lo":18.070278,"d":2062,"ds":"Lozica is a village in Croatia. It is located on the D8 state road, west of Dubrovnik, on the west coast of Rijeka Dubrovačka. The Franjo Tuđman Bridge is located east of the village.","wpu":"Lozica, Croatia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Lozica1.jpg/960px-Lozica1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Daksa (island)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.668333,"lo":18.056944,"d":2639,"ds":"Daksa is a small uninhabited island in the Croatian part of the Adriatic Sea. It is situated near Dubrovnik in front of the Rijeka Dubrovačka ria. The area of the island is about 0.07 km2 (0.027 sq mi), the highest point is 24 m (79 ft) above sea level.","wpu":"Daksa (island)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Island_of_Daksa%2C_near_Dubrovnik%2C_Croatia_%2826.10.2007.%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Via Drine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.640278,"lo":18.108333,"d":2686,"ds":"Via Drine, sometimes also Dubrovački put, was a medieval trade route through the Dinaric Alps that connected Dubrovnik (Republic of Ragusa) with the Drina river valley, and from there to various places in medieval Bosnia, Croatia and Serbia as well as the rest of the Balkans. The route went through Trebinje, Bileća, Gacko, Foča, Goražde, Višegrad, and Srebrenica.","wpu":"Via Drine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Promet_14._st.png/960px-Promet_14._st.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sponza Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.6411,"lo":18.1106,"d":2753,"ds":"The Sponza Palace (Croatian: Palača Sponza; Italian: Palazzo Sponza), also called Divona (from dogana, customs), is a 16th-century palace in Dubrovnik, Croatia. Its name is derived from the Latin word \"spongia\", the spot where rainwater was collected. The rectangular building with an inner courtyard was built in a mixed Gothic and Renaissance style between 1516 and 1522 by Paskoje Miličević Mihov.","wpu":"Sponza Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/2011_Dubrownik%2C_Pa%C5%82ac_Sponza_%2801%29.jpg/960px-2011_Dubrownik%2C_Pa%C5%82ac_Sponza_%2801%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Diocese of Dubrovnik","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.6399,"lo":18.1107,"d":2851,"ds":"The Diocese of Dubrovnik (Croatian: Dubrovačka biskupija); or Ragusa (Latin: Dioecesis Ragusiensis) is a Latin Church ecclesiastical territory or diocese of the Catholic Church in southern Croatia. The diocese is centred in the city of Dubrovnik. It was first erected in 990.","wpu":"Diocese of Dubrovnik","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/DubrovnikDSC00158.JPG/960px-DubrovnikDSC00158.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lokrum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.633333,"lo":18.116667,"d":3716,"ds":"Lokrum is an island in the Adriatic Sea 600 metres (1,969 feet) from the city of Dubrovnik, Croatia. It stretches from northwest to southeast and receives regular ferry service from the Old City port. Austrian archduke (and short-lived Emperor of Mexico) Maximilian once had a holiday home on the island.","wpu":"Lokrum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Isla_de_Lokrum%2C_Croacia%2C_2014-04-14%2C_DD_01.JPG/960px-Isla_de_Lokrum%2C_Croacia%2C_2014-04-14%2C_DD_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Zaton, Dubrovnik-Neretva County","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.690833,"lo":18.039722,"d":5257,"ds":"Zaton is a village in southern Croatia, administratively located in the City of Dubrovnik. It is located on the coast of the eponymous bay, 8 km northwest of Dubrovnik, next to the village of Orašac. Chief occupations are tourism, fishing farming, viticulture, and olive growing.","wpu":"Zaton, Dubrovnik-Neretva County","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Zaton_%28Dubrovnik%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Koločep","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":42.676389,"lo":18.006667,"d":6801,"ds":"Koločep (pronounced [kɔ̂lɔtʃɛp]; locally known as Kalamota from Italian: Calamotta) is the southernmost inhabited island in Croatia and one of the three inhabited Elaphiti Islands situated near the city of Dubrovnik. Koločep has an area of 2.44 square kilometres (0.94 square miles). According to the 2021 census, its population was 231.","wpu":"Koločep","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Calamotta.JPG/960px-Calamotta.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Dubrovnik, historically also known as Ragusa, is a city in southern Dalmatia, Croatia, by the Adriatic Sea. It is one of the most prominent tourist destinations in the Mediterranean, a seaport and the centre of the Dubrovnik-Neretva County. In 2021, its total population was 41,562.","wpu":"Dubrovnik","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/The_walls_of_the_fortress_and_View_of_the_old_city._panorama.jpg/960px-The_walls_of_the_fortress_and_View_of_the_old_city._panorama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}